    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-6600",
    ":question/problem": "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."
  },
