      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-103567",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =y\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-x{e}^{2x}$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."
